v {xschem version=3.4.8RC file_version=1.2
*
* The mini-MOSbius chip as a block: its nine real pins and nothing else
* (SPEC.md Sec 3.1b). Every design has exactly these pins, so this one
* symbol is every design's symbol -- the five ua bus pins down the left,
* the two supplies and the bias reference on the right.
*
* Which design an instance stands for is an instance attribute, not a
* separate symbol file:
*
*     C {mini_mosbius.sym} 0 0 0 0 {name=x1
*     schematic=inverter.sch}
*
* netlists as `x1 <nets> inverter`, wrapping inverter.sch's contents.
* Without a schematic= it falls back to this symbol's own schematic,
* mini_mosbius.sch -- the empty design block. That file has to exist and
* has to keep this name: xschem opens <symbol>.sch when a subcircuit
* symbol is placed, and errors in the GUI if it is missing (reported
* 2026-08-24, when this symbol was renamed without its schematic).
* The .sch EXTENSION IS LOAD-BEARING: `schematic=inverter.sch` resolves
* against the referring schematic's own directory, while a bare
* `schematic=inverter` is looked up on XSCHEM_LIBRARY_PATH instead, does
* not find a design sitting in examples/, and falls back to THIS file's
* empty body -- giving you `.subckt inverter` with no transistors in it,
* which netlists and simulates perfectly happily and answers nothing.
* Verified both ways 2026-08-23/24. If a testbench's results look
* impossibly clean, check this first.
*
* The subcircuit name follows the schematic= file, not this symbol's
* name, so the same instance can also stand for a generated netlist:
* examples/inverter/tb_inverter.sch's x2 sets schematic=inverter_routed
* with a spice_sym_def .include, and gets `.subckt inverter_routed` --
* the same design as it exists inside the real chip, from
* `mosbius simulate`.
*
* The B (pin box) records below are in netlist pin order, which is also
* the .subckt port order, and mosbius/simulate.py hard-codes that same
* order for the subcircuit it generates. Re-space or move the pins
* freely -- but never reorder these nine lines, or a design's routed
* block silently swaps two connections. Moving them DOES break any
* testbench already wired to the old positions (xschem connects a label
* by the coordinate it touches): xschem/mosbius_lib/tb_template.sch had
* to be re-wired for exactly that reason when these pins moved.
}
G {}
K {type=subcircuit
format="@name @pinlist @symname"
template="name=x1"
}
V {}
S {}
F {}
E {}
L 4 -100 -140 100 -140 {}
L 4 -100 140 100 140 {}
L 4 -100 -140 -100 140 {}
L 4 100 -140 100 140 {}
B 5 97.5 77.5 102.5 82.5 {name=ibias dir=inout}
B 5 -102.5 -82.5 -97.5 -77.5 {name=ua1 dir=inout}
B 5 -102.5 -42.5 -97.5 -37.5 {name=ua2 dir=inout}
B 5 -102.5 -2.5 -97.5 2.5 {name=ua3 dir=inout}
B 5 -102.5 37.5 -97.5 42.5 {name=ua4 dir=inout}
B 5 -102.5 77.5 -97.5 82.5 {name=ua5 dir=inout}
B 5 97.5 -102.5 102.5 -97.5 {name=VAPWR dir=inout}
B 5 97.5 -42.5 102.5 -37.5 {name=VDPWR dir=inout}
B 5 97.5 17.5 102.5 22.5 {name=VGND dir=inout}
T {mini-MOSbius} -95 -195 0 0 0.25 0.25 {}
T {@symname} -95 -160 0 0 0.3 0.3 {}
T {@name} 70 -160 0 0 0.2 0.2 {}
T {ibias} 85 70 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {ua1} -75 -90 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {ua2} -75 -50 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {ua3} -75 -10 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {ua4} -75 30 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {ua5} -75 70 0 1 0.15 0.15 {}
T {VAPWR} 65 -110 0 0 0.15 0.15 {}
T {VDPWR} 65 -50 0 0 0.15 0.15 {}
T {VGND} 65 10 0 0 0.15 0.15 {}
